  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA relatively weak shortwave is expected to cross the Upper Ohio\nValley region today. Isolated to scattered showers are expected,\nthough much of the day should be dry. By this afternoon, most of\nthe shower activity should be east of I-79, along a mid level\ntrough. Instability is progged to be minimal, with only an\nisolated thunderstorm possible. This activity should diminish\nthis evening as the shortwave exits to the east.\n\nSunday should start with dry weather ahead of another\napproaching shortwave. This wave should bring scattered showers\nand thunderstorms to portions of the area, initially north and \nwest of Pittsburgh, by afternoon into the evening. Additional \nconvergence along a lake breeze boundary could also enhance some\nof the thunderstorm coverage. Low and mid level flow is \nexpected to remain relatively weak, though an upper jet is \nexpected to approach Ohio in the afternoon. Generally a low \npotential exists for severe storms on Sunday, though DCAPE \nvalues around 750 j/kg indicate some damaging wind gusts will be\npossible. The Storm Prediction Center has included much of Ohio\nin a Marginal Risk for severe storms. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThunderstorm chances are expected to increase by Monday in\nnorthwest flow aloft. An MCS is possible across the Midwest to\nthe Ohio Valley region Sunday night into Monday morning.\nCurrent indications are this should stay west of the forecast\narea following the 500 mb thickness pattern, though this will \nneed to be monitored as details become more certain. \n\nBy afternoon, building diurnal instability and a subtle \ncrossing shortwave should result in increasing showers and \nthunderstorms, and a severe weather potential. ML CAPE is \nprogged from 1500-2500 j/kg, with increasing flow aloft \nresulting in 40 kt of 0-6km shear. The potential for severe \nstorms is expected to continue into Monday night as a stronger \nshortwave and surface cold front approach from the Great Lakes \nregion. Again, there will be a potential for an MCS somewhere in\nthe region, though low confidence continues in the location and\ntiming of this potential. \n\nThe shortwave and surface cold front is progged to cross the\nUpper Ohio Valley region on Tuesday. A severe weather potential\nis expected to continue, though this will be highly dependent on\nthe timing of passage, and any MCS activity Monday night/Tuesday\nmorning. \n\nThe latest machine learning guidance indicate the highest\npotential for severe storms should be from 12Z Monday through\n12Z Tuesday. The highest potential on Tuesday is indicated\nacross far SE OH, SW PA, and northern WV. All hazards are\npossible, with damaging wind, hail, isolated tornadoes and\nlocally heavy rainfall/flash flooding. Low confidence remains in\nthe timing/location, and how these potential hazards will be\nimpacted by previous convection, outflows, etc.",
